A||||9||MooCow||Stearing Stablizer replacement||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||08-30-2001||01:23 AM||||How do you know when you need this replaced??<BR>My car is hard to turn and when on the road at times it will go its own way.  At first I thoght it was a Fiero thing till I dorve my friends and his wheel is so easy to turn compared to mine.  Also when I let go of the wheel it will stay straight.  The only time it will go its own way is if I hit lots of bumbs or on the highway at times when I go over bumbs.  Will the stabalizer fix this??  Anything else I should look for?<P>Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Primaris||08-30-2001||02:10 AM||||I'm not sure what your talking about when you say stabelizer.  Are you talking about the sway bar?  If so this is not the cause of your wandering.  More likely a problem with your alinment.  If you mean the steering damper remove it and see if you like the lighter steering.  You will notice more impact at the steering wheel from bumps though.<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000002||tiacPaul||08-30-2001||03:28 PM||||That sounds like it could easily be the dapener.  the fiero store one is only 40.42.  I would also look at your sway bar bushings.  If that doesn't do it than it is probably the steering rack itself.||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||08-30-2001||04:00 PM||||I would remove the shock of the frt of the rack and see if it is hanging up. I have heard of them siezing and causing excessivly stiff steering.  I have the one off my wifes car and she likes it like that but I prefer it on mine.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||08-30-2001||05:05 PM||||I meant the the steering dampener sorry.  Someone told me that could be the cause of it.  I will remove and see how it handles then.  <P>       Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fformula88||08-30-2001||05:33 PM||||If its old I think I'd replace it anyway.  We replaced the one on my fathers old 87 coupe a few years ago because it was original and looked really bad.  The difference in steering was like night and day!  Before it felt really loose going over bumps like the wheels were going to come off the car.  After installing the new damper everything tightened up and it steered like new!||||reg||1
Z||000006||roadwarrior||08-30-2001||09:23 PM||||the stearing stabalizer for those of you who don't know is basically a shock mounted to your drag arm on the stearing system, a good indication that your stabalizer is bumb steer and it sounds like that's what you are describing, bump steer is the car tracks strait and true when but when there are bumps the car seems to steer itself, this is all caused by suspension geometry and all the steering stabalizer does is minimize it, so if you feel it is doing this worse than when you first got the car, replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000007||operator64||08-31-2001||01:24 AM||||   Sounds like we have the same problem. I just ordered one from the fierostore yesterday along with all new wheel bearings, i'll put the stabalizer on mine as soon as it comes in (middle of next week ) and post here how it does afterwords.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Primaris||08-31-2001||02:16 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by roadwarrior:<BR><B>the stearing stabalizer for those of you who don't know is basically a shock mounted to your drag arm on the stearing system, a good indication that your stabalizer is bumb steer and it sounds like that's what you are describing, bump steer is the car tracks strait and true when but when there are bumps the car seems to steer itself, this is all caused by suspension geometry and all the steering stabalizer does is minimize it, so if you feel it is doing this worse than when you first got the car, replace it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Roadwarrior,<BR>The steering damper does not cause bump steer; it is caused by suspension geometry like you said.  Now the damper only removes pressure/feeling to the steering wheel.  It has no bearing on the geometry of the suspension.  The front end on a Fiero has very little bump steer.  The rear suspension is a different matter, though.  Bump steer is when the suspension changes the alignment of the wheels as it moves up and down through its range of motion.<P><P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000009||deceler8||08-31-2001||11:38 AM||||I could swear I got my steering stabilizer at NAPA and it was less than $20 about a year ago.<P>||||reg||1
